WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:05.850
In this day and age of rapid-fire frame-by-frame
analysis, there's not much that goes unnoticed

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:07.140
in modern movies.

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:13.910
"Secretly I'm just like, hey man, where is
the chase and how do I cut to it?"

00:00:13.910 --> 00:00:17.630
But there've been some cinematic easter eggs
that were so subtle they slipped right by

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:22.080
us until years later, when eagle-eyed fans
finally spotted something new in film favorites.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:27.529
Here are a few movie easter eggs that went
unnoticed for a surprisingly long time.

00:00:27.529 --> 00:00:29.390
Go Red Wings?

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:34.769
It took decades for one not-so-minor detail
from John Hughes' 1986 existential comedy

00:00:34.769 --> 00:00:37.570
Ferris Bueller's Day Off to finally be explained.

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:41.870
In the movie, teen slacker Ferris takes his
girlfriend Sloan and best friend Cameron on

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:44.550
a senior skip day adventure through Chicago.

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:49.379
Together, the trio hit some of the Windy City's
most iconic sites, including Wrigley Field

00:00:49.379 --> 00:00:51.149
and the Art Institute of Chicago.

00:00:51.149 --> 00:00:55.309
All the while, Cameron wore a Detroit Red
Wings hockey jersey, which was nothing short

00:00:55.309 --> 00:00:59.100
of blasphemy since the team is an old rival
of the Chicago Blackhawks.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:04.960
It wasn't until the film's 30th Anniversary
celebration in 2016 that the reason for Cam's

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:07.900
peculiar thread selection was finally revealed.

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:11.820
Actor Alan Ruck explained that his iconic
shirt was actually a nod to the friction he

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:13.730
had with his dad in the movie.

00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:18.000
Ruck said that the director "decided that
Cameron had a horrible relationship with his

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:21.500
father, but a great relationship with his
grandfather, who lived in Detroit and could

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:23.340
take Cameron to Red Wings games.

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:26.280
That's all it was, and it was never explained
in the movie."

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:27.280
Holy Groot

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:32.711
In late 2015, Guardians of the Galaxy director
James Gunn revealed a cut image from the movie

00:01:32.711 --> 00:01:37.400
on his Facebook page in which his animated
tree hero Groot presided over a table full

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:41.060
of characters arranged just like Leonardo
Da Vinci's The Last Supper.

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:45.670
After one fan joked that Groot could "be his
own cross" because he's a tree, and thusly

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:50.640
made of wood, Gunn mentioned that the fan
wasn't far off, and there was a similarly

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:53.630
Christ-themed Easter egg that fans had not
yet notice.

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:58.390
Then someone found it: As the team's ship
is about to crash, Groot stretches his arms

00:01:58.390 --> 00:02:03.090
out in a shape resembling Christ on the cross,
shielding the other Guardians, which is a

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:06.860
pretty Christ-like gesture as well, if you
think about it.

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:08.450
Medication Memo

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:12.880
Hannibal Lecter has definitely had his share
of memorable moments over the years.

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:16.910
Arguably the most memorable of all came in
Silence of the Lambs, when he told Clarice

00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:21.400
Starling about another government official
who tried to match wits with him.

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:25.030
"A census taker once tried to test me.

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:30.470
I ate his liver with some fava beans a nice
chianti.

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:33.240
Phphphphph."

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:37.390
It may not have been obvious to everyone,
but that maniacal menu description might have

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:38.770
contained a sinister clue.

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:45.060
See, the movie was based on Thomas Harris'
1988 novel of the same name, which was published

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:48.490
at a time when a certain antidepressant was
popularly prescribed.

00:02:48.490 --> 00:02:52.819
That medication, known as an MAOI, just so
happened to have advised against patients

00:02:52.819 --> 00:02:58.610
ingesting items eerily similar to the ones
he described — namely, foods like liver,

00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:01.600
fava beans, and red wine.

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:05.490
Hannibal himself would've known this, since
he was a psychiatrist.

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:09.650
So maybe he was giving Clarice a hint that
he'd gone off his meds and planned his big

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:13.860
escape from the cell that separated him from
the rest of the world...

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:14.860
The Potato Strikes Back

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:20.130
The Star Wars franchise might just be the
most watched in history.

00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:24.600
Needless to say, over the years, keen eyes
have spotted occasional gaffes and mistakes…and

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:25.900
an in-joke or two.

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:30.310
The making of The Empire Strikes Back pushed
the cast and crew to their limits.

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:34.180
George Lucas, wanting a break from directing
and daunted by the pressure of following up

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:38.520
Star Wars, might not have directed the sequel,
but he still took a hands-on approach during

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:42.990
the production process — especially when
it came to the special effects.

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:48.400
The film's famed asteroid chase became a particular
sticking point for Lucas, who reportedly ordered

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:50.920
the effects crew to redo it several times.

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:55.480
Bored, frustrated, and inspired by a passing
comment remarking that the asteroids looked

00:03:55.480 --> 00:04:00.060
like potatoes, the effects team decided to
hide an actual potato in an asteroid field

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:01.060
during a chase scene.

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:05.100
"And I think uh Ken Ralston said 'Ah, hell,
looks like a potato.

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:06.400
We should put some potatoes in-'"

00:04:06.740 --> 00:04:08.860
"The ones in the very distant."

00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:10.440
"The ones in the very distance were actual
potatoes."

00:04:12.770 --> 00:04:16.239
Lucas himself apparently didn't notice the
prank until years later.

00:04:16.239 --> 00:04:18.230
Audiences took their time too.

00:04:18.230 --> 00:04:22.139
It wasn't until the days of video and laserdisc
that viewers could freeze-frame the scene

00:04:22.139 --> 00:04:23.560
and play spot the spud.

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:25.080
Thanks for watching!

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:28.729
Subscribe to our YouTube channel for more
videos like the one you've just watched, and

00:04:28.729 --> 00:04:31.340
let us know which little-known easter eggs
you've noticed in movies...

